'''beach''' (bēch), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''beech'', ''beatch'', ''baich'', ''baiche'', ''bache''; first in early mod. E., appar. dial., with the meaning first given. Origin unknown.] '''1.''' The loose pebbles of the seashore; shingle. [Eng.]—'''2.''' That part of the shore of the sea or of a lake which is washed by the tide and waves; the strand. {{smaller|It may be sometimes used for the shore of large rivers. It usually means the tract between high- and low-water mark.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Only the long waves as they broke
In ripples on the pebbly ''beach''.</poem>
{{right|''Longfellow'', Building of the Ship.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Raised beach''', in ''geol.'', a shelf or terrace of shingle, gravel, and sand, elevated above the sea-level, and indicating a pause in the upheaval of the land, or a depression and subsequent upheaval; the margin of an ancient sea, now inland.}}

'''beach''' (bēch), ''v.'' [< ''beach'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To run or haul up (a ship or boat) on the beach.
{{smaller block|We rowed ashore, dressed in our uniform, ''beached'' the boat, and went up to the fandango.
{{right|''R. H. Dana, Jr.'', Before the Mast, p. 281.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To land upon a beach.
{{smaller block|All that afternoon we drifted between sea and shore, and ''beached'' at sunset in a new land.
{{right|''C. W. Stoddard'', South-Sea Idyls, p. 344.}}}}

'''beach-birds''' (bēch′bėrdz), ''n. pl.'' A collective name of sundry sandpipers or other small wading birds found in flocks on beaches.
